<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Studied Code LLMs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable a fair, reproducible, and practical evaluation, we selected the studied LLMs based on several key criteria.
First, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">access to the model’s hidden states is essential</span>, as our goal is to evaluate code correctness by leveraging these internal representations. This requirement is typically met by open-source models, whereas closed-models or commercial models often restrict such access.
Second, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we focus on code-specialized LLMs</span>, which are trained or fine-tuned on large-scale code corpora. These models generally outperform general-purpose LLMs on programming tasks, leading to a more realistic and meaningful evaluation.
Third, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we consider the diversity of model architectures, model sizes, and training strategies</span> to broaden the scope of our analysis and improve the generalizability of our findings.
Finally, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we limit the model size to a maximum of 13 billion parameters</span> to accommodate hardware resource constraints and ensure that our evaluation is practical and reproducible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these criteria, we selected six representative LLMs from different model families to ensure both architectural diversity and broad generalizability of our evaluation, including <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek Coder</span>-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek Coder</span>-6.7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Llama</span>-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Llama</span>-13B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Magicoder</span>-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Gemma</span>-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.
All selected models are open-source, allowing full access to their internal representations during code generation.
These models are widely adopted in both academic research and industrial applications, and have demonstrated strong performance on SE tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
Each employed model is either trained or fine-tuned on large-scale code corpora to capture the syntactic, semantic, and logical aspects of programming languages.
For consistency, we use the instruction-tuned versions with officially released pre-trained weights from HuggingFace and do not perform further fine-tuning. This setup enables a fair assessment of each model’s generalization ability and facilitates a controlled comparison of how their internal representations relate to code correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the performance of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> against SOTA <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">white-box</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box</span> approaches. White-box approaches analyze the model’s internal computations during code generation to determine the correctness of the generated output. In contrast, black-box approaches evaluate the code correctness based solely on the final generated output without considering the generation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">White-box methods</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>: These methods utilize internal signals such as attention maps, hidden activations, or internal representations as indicators of correctness. In this study, we evaluate the effectiveness of <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> by comparing it against a SOTA white-box method <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and an <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span> baseline.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> determines if LLM-generated code is (in)correct by training a probing classifier using the internal states from the last generated token at the last layer of the models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span> represents an upper-bound baseline constructed by exhaustively searching across all layers and token positions to identify the <span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">most optimal</span> internal representations of each model for each evaluation setting. Since the search space grows exponentially with the number of layers and tokens, such a full search is computationally prohibitive and infeasible in practice. In this research, we approximate this oracle by individually evaluating representations from four boundary tokens (<span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">first token</span>, <span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">last token</span>, <span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">first code token</span>, <span id="S4.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">last code token</span>) across layers, providing a manageable yet informative estimation of the optimal choice.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Black-box methods</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>: We consider two representative black-box strategies:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Post-hoc classification-based methods</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>: These methods predict correct code by considering the code semantics, typically captured using a pretrained model for code, e.g., CodeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, CodeT5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, or CuBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. In this work, we employed two widely used models, including CodeBERT and CodeT5+, to encode the code semantics and train a classifier on the resulting embeddings to determine correctness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-A-Judge (LLM-AJ)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>: These methods evaluate code correctness by utilizing the reasoning and generalization capabilities of LLMs. Following the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, we adopt a zero-shot prompting strategy with two configurations: <span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span>, where the same model, that generates code, is used to assess its own output, and <span id="S4.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span>, where an external LLM (i.e., o4-mini) serves as a verifier. The full prompt templates used in our experiments are available on our website <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Evaluation Procedure</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1. Performance analysis:</span>
We evaluate <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> and the baselines across three key dimensions: <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compilability</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functionality</span>, and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">security</span> under different settings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Independent-unit code generation<span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">:</span></span> This focuses on standalone programming tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. For each studied LLM, the classifier is trained on internal representations extracted from one benchmark (e.g., MBPP) and evaluated on another (e.g., HumanEval). This setting tests the generalizability of correctness assessment across different benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Repo(sitory)-level code generation<span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">:</span></span> This setting evaluates the code correctness in the context of real-world software projects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Repo-level tasks require LLMs to maintain contextual consistency across multiple code units, significantly increasing the complexity of code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This setting measures how robust the approaches are in handling context-dependent code generation scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a fair comparison, we use the same architecture for the classification models in the post-hoc methods, <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span>, and <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>. Each classifier contains an input layer, two hidden layers with 128 and 64 neurons respectively, and an output layer. All models are trained for 50 epochs using a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2. Intrinsic Analysis:</span> We investigate how different components of <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> contribute to its overall performance. To answer this question, we systematically evaluate <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> under different configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Internal Representation Extractor</span>, we aim to examine the impact of different sampling strategies:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Token sampling</span>: We compare <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance under three sampling strategies: (1) <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full token</span>, (2) <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">random</span>, and (3) <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">boundary-aware</span>. <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full token</span> uses representations from all generated tokens. To accommodate varying sequence lengths, we fix the sequence size to 256 tokens and apply padding or truncation as needed. <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random</span> randomly selects one interior token position. <span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Boundary-aware</span> selects four key tokens at boundary positions (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Internal Representation Extractor ‣ 3 Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Layer sampling</span>: We evaluate the impacts of sampling different subsets of layers by varying the sampling intervals, <math id="S4.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which defines the step size between selected layers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Informative Representation Selector</span>, we conduct an ablation study to investigate its contribution to <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance. Specifically, we compare <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance under two configurations:</p>
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With Selector</span>: This module is <span id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">enabled</span> to assign importance scores to each internal representation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Without Selector</span>: The selector module is <span id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">disabled</span>. All internal representations are uniformly aggregated with equal importance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Code Correctness Predictor</span>, we study how the choice of aggregation function and probing classifier affects the overall results. Regarding the <span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">aggregation function</span>, we evaluate several strategies for combining the selected internal representations <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, including concatenation, summation, mean pooling, and max pooling. For the <span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probing classifier</span>, we explore various classifier architectures, including Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Logistic Regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">All intrinsic analysis experiments are conducted using <span id="S4.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Gemma</span>-7B on the MBPP benchmark. For each experiment, the MBPP tasks are randomly split into a 90:10 ratio for training and testing.
Internal representations extracted during the code generation process for the tasks in the training set are used to train the probing classifier, while those from the test set are used to evaluate its performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3. Sensitivity analysis:</span>
This experiment examines <span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s generalization capability across programming languages and LLMs.
Specifically, we assess whether <span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>, when trained on internal representations extracted from code generation in certain programming languages, can accurately predict the correctness of code written in a completely different language.
We also analyze how varying the size of the training data affects <span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s prediction performance.
Additionally, we examine <span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s ability to transfer across models by training it on the internal states of several code LLMs and testing its performance on code generated by a different model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determining whether code generated by an LLM is correct can be formulated as a binary classification problem.
To evaluate <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> and the baseline methods, we employ standard classification metrics.
For a fair evaluation across both classes, especially in the presence of class imbalance, we adopt weighted metrics including <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accuracy</span>, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weighted Precision</span>, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weighted Recall</span>, and <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weighted F1-Score</span>.
These metrics compute a label-wise average weighted by the proportion of each class in the test set.
We also evaluate the efficiency of each approach by measuring time complexity (for both training and inference) and memory usage. These metrics provide a comprehensive understanding of the practical feasibility of the approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

