<td id="S5.T4.6.43.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T4.6.43.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.88</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.6.43.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T4.6.43.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.88</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.6.43.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T4.6.43.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.88</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.6.43.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T4.6.43.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.88</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The performance of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> and the baseline approaches in code correctness assessment under the security criterion is shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1.1 Security Assessment ‣ 5.1 Performance Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Overall, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> consistently outperforms all baselines across all studied code LLMs. Notably, </span>AutoProbe<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">’s performance closely matches or even exceeds that of </span>Oracle<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, indicating its effectiveness in capturing informative internal signals.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the white-box approach <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> yields improvement gains of up to 18%. For example, when assessing code generated by <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Llama</span>-13B, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> reaches an accuracy of 0.88, while this figure of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span> is only 0.75. This performance gap can be attributed to the limitations of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span>’s design, which relies on fixed representations from the last generated token at the last layer of the LLM. Such a rigid strategy hinders its flexibility and generalizability across different model architectures.
In contrast, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> dynamically selects the most informative internal states, allowing it to tailor the selected representations to each model’s characteristics. This flexible mechanism enables <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> to capture correctness-related signals more effectively, resulting in more robust performance across diverse models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> achieve considerably low performance. The accuracy of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span> lags behind <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> by margins ranging from 24% to 133%.
In the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span> setting, the same LLM that generates the code is also used to assess its own outputs, making it prone to overconfidence in the correctness of its responses. Similarly, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> falls short of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> by 19% to 82%, even when using a powerful external model like o4-mini.
This performance gap underscores the challenge of detecting security vulnerabilities, which often require deep semantic understanding and context-aware reasoning.
These results expose the limitations of using LLMs directly as correctness judges under the security criterion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The back-box classification approaches based on CodeBERT and CodeT5+ show quite strong performance across all code LLMs in evaluating the security correctness. For instance, the accuracies achieved by CodeT5+ are comparable to <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span> and better than <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> by up to 55%. However, their performance still lags behind <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> from 5% to 14%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> exhibits strong performance, which is close to <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span>.
Interestingly, on <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek Coder</span>-1.3B, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> achieves an accuracy of 0.90, which slightly surpasses <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span>’s performance. By aggregating the sampled and weighted internal representations, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> is able to not only focus on the most informative states but also integrate complementary information across multiple tokens and layers. This mechanism enhances its ability to detect security vulnerabilities. As a result, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> can outperform <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span>, which relies on a single representation in some cases.
These results highlight the effectiveness of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> in capturing important signals for security correctness prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
