<td id="S5.T6.6.39.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T6.6.39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.39.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.44</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.39.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r">0.27</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.39.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.52</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.39.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.36</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.6.40" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.6.40.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T6.6.40.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.40.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.88</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.40.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r">0.86</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.40.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.55</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.40.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.49</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.6.41" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.6.41.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r">CodeBERT</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.41.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.81</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.41.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r">0.82</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.41.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.58</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.41.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.56</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.6.42" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.6.42.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r">CodeT5+</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.42.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.78</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.42.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r">0.80</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.42.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.61</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.42.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.60</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.6.43" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.6.43.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T6.6.43.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Openia</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.43.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.84</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.43.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r">0.79</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.43.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.67</td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.43.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.67</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.6.44" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T6.6.44.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T6.6.44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.44.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.6.44.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.90</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.44.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T6.6.44.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.89</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.44.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.6.44.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.69</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.6.44.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T6.6.44.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.69</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.1.3 Compilability Assessment ‣ 5.1 Performance Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the results of the compilability assessment under two levels of code generation granularity, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent-unit</span> code and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repo-level</span> code. As seen, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">most correctness assessment approaches achieve strong performance in independent-unit code, yet show considerable decline in the more complex repo-level setting</span>. Among them, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> demonstrates the highest robustness, maintaining the smallest performance drop between the two settings.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, in the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent-unit</span> setting, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> achieves the highest performance, with an average accuracy of 0.92, which is 69% higher than <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">In-house LLM-AJ</span> and 11% higher than black-box classification with CodeT5+. However, its performance drops by 1.5 times, reaching an average accuracy of 0.59 in the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repo-level</span> setting.
Indeed, determining whether a piece of code is compilable is mainly dependent on checking syntax and structural correctness, without the need for deep reasoning.
In <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent-unit</span> snippets, all relevant information, such as imports, declared variables, and function definitions, is self-contained, allowing LLMs like o4-mini to simply scan the code to verify its syntactic validity.
Meanwhile, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repo-level</span> code often references variables, functions, or modules defined across multiple files. Without explicit access to the entire project context, the LLM struggles to accurately determine compilability, leading to a significant performance drop in this setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the more complex setting of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repo-level</span> code assessment, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> achieves the highest performance, outperforming <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> by 12% on average.
For example, when evaluating the compilability of code generated by <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Code Gemma</span>-7B, both <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span> reach the accuracy of 0.90 on <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">independent-unit</span> code.
However, for <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repo-level</span> code, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> obtains an accuracy of 0.69, which is 27% higher than that of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">External LLM-AJ</span>. Interestingly, in this case, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> even slightly surpasses the performance of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Oracle</span>.
These results demonstrate that <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> is less sensitive to the increased code complexity, making it a more reliable choice for practical correctness assessment scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{mdtheorem}</span>
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">namelabel
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to RQ1</span>: Across all three correctness dimensions, security, functionality, and compilability, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> demonstrates strong and consistent performance, outperforming SOTA baselines.</span>
</span></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Intrinsic Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Impact of Representation Sampling Strategies</h4>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02934v1/token_sampling.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of token sampling on <span id="S5.F4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance and memory usage, left axis: <span id="S5.F4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1-Score</span>; right axis: <span id="S5.F4.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Memory usage (GB)</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02934v1/layer_sampling.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of layer sampling intervals <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S5.F5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance and memory usage, left axis: <span id="S5.F5.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1-Score</span>; right axis: <span id="S5.F5.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Memory usage (GB)</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token sampling:</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2.1 Impact of Representation Sampling Strategies ‣ 5.2 Intrinsic Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the impact of different token sampling strategies on <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance. Overall, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the boundary-aware strategy offers the best balance, achieving the highest F1-Score with relatively low memory consumption.</span>
While random token sampling results in the lowest memory usage (about 6.5 GB across GPU and CPU), it yields the weakest performance. This is expected because randomly selecting a single token may overlook important signals, leading to suboptimal predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> with boundary-aware token sampling consumes 2.5 times more memory than random sampling, but achieves significantly better performance. Specifically, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> reaches an F1-Score of 0.76, outperforming full token and random token sampling strategies by 9% and 26%, respectively. Moreover, compared to full token sampling, the boundary-aware strategy reduces memory usage by 18X and training time by over 100X.
These results suggest that using all tokens introduces substantial computational overhead and even degrades performance due to the inclusion of noisy and irrelevant representations. Meanwhile, the boundary-aware strategy effectively reduces input size while preserving the most informative signals for correctness assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer Sampling:</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2.1 Impact of Representation Sampling Strategies ‣ 5.2 Intrinsic Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows how the layer sampling interval <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., how frequently layers are selected for extracting internal states, affects <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance and memory usage.
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Across different sampling intervals, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s F1-Scores are relatively stable</span>, fluctuating between 0.76 and 0.79.
This stability can be explained by the fact that adjacent layers in transformer-based models tend to encode highly similar features, with similarity increasing as layers get closer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>. As a result, densely sampling layers (small <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) often captures redundant features without providing additional information for correctness prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">memory usage decreases steadily as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases,</span> since larger intervals result in fewer selected layers and a smaller set of extracted representations <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby lowering memory consumption.
For example, when <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span> requires about 60 GB of GPU and CPU, which is 4.7X more than when <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results highlight the importance of selecting a representative subset of layers rather than using all of them, which increases overhead without improving code correctness prediction performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Impact of Informative Representation Selector</h4>

<figure id="S5.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="S5.T7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of <span id="S5.T7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Informative Representation Selector</span> on <span id="S5.T7.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s Performance</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T7.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:45pt;vertical-align:-19.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(30.0pt,-3.9pt) scale(1.21017093122742,1.21017093122742) ;">
<table id="S5.T7.7.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T7.7.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt">Accuracy</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt">Precision</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt">Recall</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt">F1-Score</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T7.7.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Without selector</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">0.70</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">0.70</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">0.70</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">0.69</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T7.7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">With selector</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.76</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.76</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.76</td>
<td id="S5.T7.7.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.76</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.2.2 Impact of Informative Representation Selector ‣ 5.2 Intrinsic Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Model-Agnostic Correctness Assessment for LLM-Generated Code via Dynamic Internal Representation Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates that <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the informative representation selector module significantly contributes to <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s performance</span>.
Indeed, not all internal representations contribute equally to correctness prediction. Without a mechanism to identify and prioritize useful signals, the classifier struggles to capture important information.
By incorporating an attention mechanism to assign higher weights to the important representations and downweight the irrelevant ones, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s F1-Score in code correctness prediction improves from 0.69 to 0.76 (more than 10% relatively).
The selector module thus plays a crucial role in directing the model’s focus toward the most relevant internal states, leading to more effective and reliable correctness assessment.
Moreover, since the attention scores are jointly trained with the probing classifier, the training time remains comparable with and without the selector module.
This suggests that the performance improvement comes at a reasonable computational cost, making the selector a lightweight yet useful addition to the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AutoProbe</span>’s design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.3 </span>Impact of Aggregation and Probing Classier</h4>

<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
